## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level synchronization and memory-order reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Ticket-based spinlock yields FIFO fairness
- Atomics: fetch_add, load, store
- Memory orders: acquire on lock, release on unlock, relaxed during waiting

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  _Atomic uint32_t next;
  _Atomic uint32_t serve;
} TicketLock;
void init(TicketLock* t){ atomic_store(&t->next,0); atomic_store(&t->serve,0); }
uint32_t lock(TicketLock* t){ uint32_t my = atomic_fetch_add(&t->next,1); while (atomic_load(&t->serve) != my) { _mm_pause(); } return my; }
void unlock(TicketLock* t, uint32_t my){ atomic_store_explicit(&t->serve, my+1, memory_order_release); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for NUMA or many threads?
- What are practical drawbacks of busy-waiting and how would you verify FIFO fairness under stress?

## Why This Is Asked
Low-level memory management on constrained hardware.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size allocator (slab)
- Free-list stored inside blocks; index-based chaining
- O(1) allocations/deallocations with minimal metadata
- Alignment, memory overhead, and fragmentation considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct {
  uint32_t next; // index of next free slot
} FreeSlot;

#define POOL_SIZE 256
#define SLOT_SIZE 64
static uint8_t pool[POOL_SIZE * SLOT_SIZE];
static uint32_t head = 0; // index of first free slot
static uint32_t used = 0;

void slab_init(void) {
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < POOL_SIZE - 1; ++i) {
    ((uint32_t*)pool)[i] = i + 1;
  }
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[POOL_SIZE - 1] = UINT32_MAX;
  head = 0;
  used = 0;
}

void* slab_alloc(void) {
  if (head == UINT32_MAX) return NULL;
  uint32_t idx = head;
  head = ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx];
  ++used;
  return pool + idx * SLOT_SIZE;
}

void slab_free(void* p) {
  ptrdiff_t idx = ((uint8_t*)p - pool) / SLOT_SIZE;
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx] = head;
  head = (uint32_t)idx;
  --used;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect fragmentation in this design?
- How would you extend this to multi-threaded contexts with minimal locking?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of lock-free design, cross-socket contention, and memory ordering on MESI-based systems.

## Key Concepts
- Per-core local buffers to minimize cross-socket traffic and reduce coherence domain contention
- Per-slot sequence counters to prevent ABA race conditions
- Correct memory ordering: release for publishing, acquire for consuming, ensuring proper visibility across threads
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Wrap-around handling via power-of-two masking combined with sequence counter verification
- Backpressure mechanisms to handle slow consumers gracefully

## Data Structures

```c
typedef struct {
    uint64_t seq;
    char pad[64];  // align to cache line boundary
    void* data;
    char payload[112];  // 128-byte slots total
} Slot;

typedef struct {
    Slot slots[1 << 18];
    _Atomic(uint64_t) head;
    _Atomic(uint64_t) tail;
    char pad[L1_CACHE_SIZE];
} Ring;

typedef struct {
    char buffer[1 << 12][128];
    uint64_t seq[1 << 12];
    uint32_t local_head;
    char pad[L1_CACHE_SIZE];
} PerCoreQueue;
```

## Pseudocode

**Enqueue:**
```c
bool enqueue(Ring* ring, PerCoreQueue* local, void* data, size_t len) {
    uint32_t slot = local->local_head & (1 << 12 - 1);

    memcpy(local->buffer[slot], data, len);
    atomic_store_explicit(&local->seq[slot], 
                          local->seq[slot] + 2, 
                          memory_order_relaxed);

    uint64_t desired = local->local_head + 1;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
            &ring->tail, &desired, desired,
            memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed)) {
        local->local_head++;
        return true;
    }
    return false;  // backpressure
}
```

**Dequeue:**
```c
bool dequeue(Ring* ring, void* out, size_t* len) {
    uint64_t head = atomic_load_explicit(&ring->head, memory_order_relaxed);

    uint64_t desired = head + 1;
    if (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
            &ring->head, &head, desired,
            memory_order_acquire, memory_order_relaxed)) {
        return false;
    }

    Slot* slot = &ring->slots[head & (N - 1)];
    uint64_t seq = atomic_load_explicit(&slot->seq, memory_order_acquire);
    if ((seq & 1) != 0) return false;  // not yet written

    memcpy(out, slot->data, *len);
    atomic_store_explicit(&slot->seq, seq + 1, memory_order_release);
    return true;
}
```

## Microbenchmark Plan

1. **Throughput measurement:** Vary producer/consumer thread counts (1-16), measure ops/sec at fixed load.

2. **Cache-coherence traffic:** Use perf counters (`perf stat -e cache-references,cache-misses, LLC-load-misses, LLC-store-misses`) across:
   - Single-socket baseline (no cross-socket traffic)
   - Multi-socket with threads pinned to different NUMA nodes
   - Vary write-to-read ratios (100/0, 50/50, 0/100)

3. **Contention stress test:** Saturate ring capacity, measure under overflow conditions.

4. **Baseline comparison:** Compare against mutex-protected ring and lock-free SPSC queue.
